# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS, AND WHY `.env` DOES NOT COVER IT.
#
# @nuxt/test-utils builds the app for the `nuxt` vitest project with
# `dotenv: { fileName: ".env.test" }` — it never reads `.env`. Without this file
# `nuxt.config.ts` was evaluated with no variables at all: `image.domains` came
# out empty, so banner artwork skipped IPX, and the catalogue image origin came
# out empty too, so product URLs stayed relative — and a relative URL is exactly
# what IPX does transform. Both expectations ended up inverted, and the suite's
# result depended on whatever each developer happened to keep in `.env`.
#
# Hosts on the reserved `.test` TLD: nothing is ever fetched, they only have to
# be STABLE. And both variables below name the SAME host, as they do in
# production, where Supabase is served behind the API's own domain
# (`https://nowtech-api.newvcreatives.com/supabase`). The suite used to keep the
# two apart, which let product images skip IPX only because their host was
# missing from `image.domains` — the one arrangement production no longer has.
# With a shared host, a product-image test passes only because the components
# opt out of IPX themselves (`products/Picture.vue`, `provider="none"`).

NUXT_IMAGE_PROVIDER=ipx

# The Supabase URL, path prefix included as in production. `nuxt.config.ts`
# keeps only its origin, which is the host `bannerImageOrigins` grants IPX
# transformation. Unprefixed on purpose: `NUXT_SUPABASE_URL` would also feed
# `runtimeConfig.supabaseUrl` and wake up real Supabase authentication, which
# `.env` already takes care to avoid.
SUPABASE_URL=https://api.test/supabase

# The catalogue backend origin — the same host as above, therefore INSIDE
# `image.domains`. Product images must still come out untransformed.
NUXT_PUBLIC_CATALOGUE_IMAGE_ORIGIN=https://api.test
